<BaseLayout title="What is MCP? A beginner's guide — FreeMCP" description="Understand the Model Context Protocol, how MCP clients and servers interact, and why developers use it.">
<article class="container info-page"><div class="eyebrow">FREEMCP / LEARN</div><h1>What is MCP? A beginner's guide</h1><p class="intro">Understand the Model Context Protocol, how MCP clients and servers interact, and why developers use it.</p><div class="info-content"><h2>What is the Model Context Protocol?</h2><p>MCP is an open protocol that standardizes how AI applications connect to external context and capabilities. Instead of building a different integration pattern for every AI client, developers can expose compatible tools, resources, and prompts through an MCP server.</p><h2>How does it work?</h2><p>An AI application acts as a host. It manages one or more MCP clients, each of which communicates with an MCP server. The server exposes capabilities the application can discover and, when authorized, use.</p><h2>Tools, resources, and prompts</h2><p><strong>Tools</strong> allow an application to invoke actions. <strong>Resources</strong> expose contextual data. <strong>Prompts</strong> provide reusable interaction templates. Availability depends on the server and client.</p><h2>Local and remote servers</h2><p>Some servers run locally and communicate over standard input/output (stdio). Others run remotely using Streamable HTTP. Follow the documentation for the particular server and client.</p><h2>Is MCP safe?</h2><p>MCP is an integration protocol, not a security guarantee. A server may access sensitive systems or execute actions. Review permissions, credentials, and source code before connecting it.</p><h2>Next steps</h2><p>Read <a href="/guides/install-mcp-server/">how to install an MCP server</a>, explore <a href="/servers/">the FreeMCP directory</a>, and consult the <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io">official MCP documentation</a>.</p></div></article>
</BaseLayout>
